  {
   "title": "Areas",
   "span": "full",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "rules",
     "head": [
      "Way",
      "Example"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "No formula: **cut** it into shapes you know",
       "house: $36+12=48$"
      ],
      [
       "Or a **big shape minus** the missing piece",
       "L shape: $60-18=42$"
      ],
      [
       "Regular hexagon: $6\\cdot\\frac{a^2\\sqrt3}{4}$",
       "$a=4$: $6\\cdot4\\sqrt3=24\\sqrt3$"
      ],
      [
       "Regular octagon: $2a^2+2a^2\\sqrt2$",
       "$a=2$: $8+8\\sqrt2$"
      ],
      [
       "Star partition: a corner triangle $=\\frac16$, $ACE=\\frac12$",
       "$a=4$: corner triangle $4\\sqrt3$"
      ]
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Meeting at a point",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Two regular polygons on a common side: first $360°$ **around the shared corner**, then the **isosceles triangle** (both of its sides = the common side). Polygons fill a point when their angles make exactly $360°$: 3 hexagons, 4 squares, 6 equilateral triangles; 3 pentagons leave $36°$."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Before each question, ask",
   "class": "check",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "checklist",
     "items": [
      "Is the polygon regular? Only then are all the angles equal.",
      "Which angle is it: the polygon's angle, the exterior angle or the central angle?",
      "Which triangles hide here: a corner triangle, isosceles triangles from radii, equilateral ones in a hexagon?",
      "An area with no formula: how do I cut it, or what do I take away?"
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "⚠ Common traps",
   "class": "traps",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "traps",
     "items": [
      "Dividing the angle sum by $n$ when the polygon is not regular.",
      "From the sum: $n-2$ is the number of triangles, not the number of sides.",
      "Stopping at one triangle of the hexagon ($\\frac16$ of the area).",
      "Counting every diagonal twice, or counting the sides as diagonals.",
      "Forgetting to halve: $30°$ is both base angles together.",
      "A polygon in a circle is not necessarily regular."
     ]
    }
   ]
  }
